(Logarithmic decrement delta, \(4\) Quality factor Q, \(5\) Time required for vibration) Tj
T*
(amplitude to drop to 10% of initial value.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Natural Frequency omega_0:) Tj
T*
(omega_0 = sqrt\(k / m\) = sqrt\(100 / 0.25\) = sqrt\(400\) = 20.0 rad/s.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Damping Factor gamma and Damped Frequency omega_d:) Tj
T*
(gamma = b / \(2 * m\) = 0.50 / \(2 * 0.25\) = 1.0 s^-1.) Tj
T*
(omega_d = sqrt\( omega_0^2 - gamma^2 \) = sqrt\( 20^2 - 1^2 \) = sqrt\(399\) = 19.975 rad/s.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Logarithmic Decrement delta:) Tj
T*
(Damped period T_d = 2 * pi / omega_d = 2 * 3.14159 / 19.975 = 0.3145 s.) Tj
T*
(delta = gamma * T_d = 1.0 * 0.3145 = 0.3145.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 4: Quality Factor Q:) Tj
T*
(Q = omega_0 / \(2 * gamma\) = 20.0 / \(2 * 1.0\) = 10.0.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 5: Time for 90% Amplitude Decay \(A\(t\) / A_0 = 0.10\):) Tj
T*
(A\(t\) = A_0 * e^\(- gamma * t\) => e^\(- 1.0 * t\) = 0.10.) Tj
T*
(- t = ln\(0.10\) = - 2.3026 => t = 2.30 seconds.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: omega_d = 19.98 rad/s; delta = 0.315; Q = 10.0; decay time to 10% amplitude) Tj
T*
(= 2.30 s.) Tj
