/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Context: In an acoustic grating experiment, an ultrasonic wave of frequency f =) Tj
T*
(3.0 MHz is set up in an organic chemical solvent. A monochromatic sodium light beam) Tj
T*
(\(lambda = 589.3 nm\) passes perpendicularly through the liquid cell, producing) Tj
T*
(diffraction fringes on a screen placed at focal distance D = 1.2 m. The distance between) Tj
T*
(the central zeroth-order and first-order diffraction maxima is x_1 = 3.5 mm. Calculate:) Tj
T*
(\(1\) Grating element d \(acoustic wavelength in liquid\), \(2\) Acoustic velocity v of) Tj
T*
(ultrasound in the solvent.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Grating Equation for First-Order Diffraction:) Tj
T*
(Diffraction angle: sin\(theta\) ~= tan\(theta\) = x_1 / D = 0.0035 m / 1.2 m = 0.002917) Tj
T*
(radians.) Tj
T*
(Grating condition: d * sin\(theta\) = 1 * lambda => d = lambda / sin\(theta\).) Tj
T*
(Acoustic wavelength d = \(589.3 * 10^-9 m\) / 0.002917 = 2.0205 * 10^-4 m = 0.202 mm.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Compute Ultrasonic Velocity v in the liquid:) Tj
T*
(v = f * d = \(3.0 * 10^6 s^-1\) * \(2.0205 * 10^-4 m\) = 606.15 m/s.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Grating pitch d = 0.202 mm; Ultrasonic wave velocity in liquid = 606.15 m/s.) Tj
T*
